 ],
 "idea": [
  "Think about the **last move** that lands on stair `i`. It was either a single step from stair `i − 1` or a double step from stair `i − 2` — there is no other possibility.",
  "So every way to reach stair `i − 1` and every way to reach stair `i − 2` gives exactly one way to reach stair `i`: `ways(i) = ways(i − 1) + ways(i − 2)`.",
  "Start from the two obvious answers (stair 1: 1 way, stair 2: 2 ways) and work upward. Only the last two values are ever needed, so two variables are enough."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def climbStairs(self, n: int) -> int:\n        if n <= 2:\n            return n\n\n        prev1 = 1  # Number of ways to reach the 1st stair\n        prev2 = 2  # Number of ways to reach the 2nd stair\n\n        for i in range(3, n + 1):\n            current = prev1 + prev2\n            prev1, prev2 = prev2, current  # Update for the next iteration\n\n        return prev2  # Number of ways to reach the n-th stair",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "One addition per stair.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "Only the last two counts are kept."
 },
